A minimal `Hello` could carry the peer’s supported protocol versions:

```rust
pub enum Msg {
    Ping,
    Hello { versions: Vec<u8> },
}
```

A simple wire representation could be:

```text
v1:hello:1
v1:hello:1,2
```

Here, `v1` identifies the message encoding format, while the comma-separated values identify protocol versions the peer supports. A corresponding sketch would be:

```rust
pub fn decode(bytes: &str) -> Result<Msg, String> {
    if let Some(versions) = bytes.strip_prefix("v1:hello:") {
        let versions = versions
            .split(',')
            .map(|value| value.parse::<u8>().map_err(|_| "invalid version".to_owned()))
            .collect::<Result<Vec<_>, _>>()?;

        if versions.is_empty() {
            return Err("missing versions".into());
        }

        return Ok(Msg::Hello { versions });
    }

    // Existing Ping handling remains unchanged.
    match bytes {
        "ping" | "v1:ping" => Ok(Msg::Ping),
        _ => Err("unknown".into()),
    }
}
```

Encoding could use the same format:

```rust
Msg::Hello { versions } => {
    let versions = versions.iter().map(u8::to_string).collect::<Vec<_>>();
    format!("v1:hello:{}", versions.join(","))
}
```

This keeps the current protocol version at `1`; adding `Hello` does not require introducing v2.

